course: medicine
lesson: 27
title: The optician and glasses
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your eye is like a camera: the clear front and the lens bend light so it focuses in a sharp picture on the retina at the back.
explain: In a short-sighted eye the picture lands just in front of the retina, so the board at school is a blur while the book on your desk is crisp.
explain: An optometrist finds out with letter charts, lights and lenses, and glasses bend the light a little more or less so the picture lands right on the retina again.
explain: Glasses don't weaken your eyes or make them lazy; many children wear them, and the first day with them can feel like someone turned up the sharpness of the whole world.
fact: Short sight, or myopia, means far things look blurry and near things clear, because the eye focuses light in front of the retina.
fact: An optometrist tests vision with a letter chart whose lines get smaller, then looks into the eye and uses lenses to find the right prescription.
fact: Glasses lenses bend light so it focuses on the retina, and wearing them does not make eyes worse.
fact: Children's prescriptions often change as they grow, so regular eye tests matter.
fact: In the UK children under 16 get free NHS eye tests and vouchers towards glasses, and tests are worth having every year or two even without a problem.
fact: A lazy eye, or amblyopia, means one eye has not developed sharp vision, and covering the good eye with a patch makes the brain use the weaker one; treatment works best in early childhood, and if it's left untreated the weak eye's sight can stay poor for life.
fact: A squint, or strabismus, is when the eyes don't line up, and orthoptists, eye workers who help with squints and lazy eyes, may suggest glasses, patches or an operation.
fact: Eye drops can widen the pupils so the optometrist can see inside the eye, and the blurriness wears off after a few hours; wide pupils let in more light, so bright sun can feel dazzling until then.
fact: Colour blindness is usually inherited, is much more common in boys, and most often makes red and green hard to tell apart.
fact: A cataract is when the lens inside the eye becomes cloudy, usually with age, and surgeons replace it with a clear plastic lens in a short operation; cataracts are not catching and don't spread like a cold.
fact: Teasing about glasses is unkind and teachers deal with it; glasses are common and many famous people wear them.
exercise: In short sight, what looks blurry? | Far things | Near things | Everything, always | Only colours
exercise: Where does the picture land in a short-sighted eye? | In front of the retina | Exactly on the retina | Behind the back of the eye | On the eyelid
exercise: What does the letter chart test? | How sharp your eyesight is | How fast you can read out loud | How good your spelling is | Your colour vision only
exercise: How do glasses help? | Their lenses bend light to focus on the retina | They make the eye muscles stronger | They block out bright light | They clean the eyes
exercise: Will wearing glasses make your eyes worse? | No | Yes, they make eyes lazy | Yes, after a year | Only if you wear them at night
exercise: Why do children need regular eye tests? | Prescriptions often change as they grow | Eyes wear out every year and need replacing | To get new frames free | Because glasses expire
exercise: Who gets free NHS eye tests? | Children under 16 | Only adults over 60 | Nobody | Only children with glasses
exercise: Why does a child wear a patch over his good eye? | To make the brain use the weaker eye | To protect the good eye from catching germs | Because the good eye is tired | To look like a pirate
exercise: What is a squint? | When the eyes don't line up | When you need to squeeze your eyes in sun | When one eye is a different colour | When eyes water a lot
exercise: Why did the optician put drops in your eyes? | To widen the pupils and see inside | To wash out dust | To change your eye colour for a few hours | To make you sleepy
exercise: Do eye-test drops damage your eyes? | No, the blur wears off in hours | Yes, they blur things for a week | Yes, they damage your eyes forever | Only if you blink while they go in
exercise: Who is colour blindness more common in? | Boys | Girls | Grown-ups only | Babies only
exercise: What colours do colour-blind people most often mix up? | Red and green | Black and white | Blue and yellow always | All colours
exercise: What is a cataract? | A cloudy lens inside the eye | A waterfall in the eye | A scratch across the glasses lens | A lazy eye
exercise: How are cataracts treated? | The cloudy lens is replaced with a plastic one | With carrot juice | With a patch | They can't be treated
exercise: What should you do if someone calls you four-eyes again and again? | Tell an adult and wear your glasses with pride | Stop wearing glasses | Call them names back | Hide in the toilet
test: Grandma: "don't wear your glasses all the time, your eyes'll get lazy". True? | No, glasses don't weaken eyes | Yes, take them off as often as you can | Yes, glasses slowly ruin your eyes | Only in summer, when it's sunny
test: Zak squints at the board but reads his book fine. What might he have? | Short sight | Long sight | Colour blindness | A cataract
test: You guess letters to "pass" the eye test. What happens? | You might not get glasses you need | You get a prize | Your eyes improve | Nothing, because guessing is totally fine
test: "Colour-blind = sees in black and white" — right? | No, they see colours but miss some differences | Yes, like an old film | Yes, but only at night | No, they see only red
test: Little bro hates his patch and takes it off. Why should he keep it on? | It trains the brain to use his weaker eye | It keeps germs out | It's a punishment | It stops him squinting at the sun
test: After drops, bright sunlight hurts your eyes. Why? | Wide pupils let in more light | The drops burn | Your eyes got weaker | The sun is brighter after tests
test: Could a cataract spread from one eye to the other like a cold? | No, cataracts don't spread | Yes, by touching the other eye | Yes, by crying with both eyes | Only in old people, who catch it
test: What would happen to a lazy eye left untreated in early childhood? | Its sight could stay weak for life | It fixes itself always | It slowly becomes the stronger, good eye | It turns blue
test: Your eyes go a bit crossed when tired and you see double. What to do? | Tell a grown-up, it should be checked | Ignore it, it'll go away on its own soon | Rub them hard | Watch more TV
test: Sasha wants to know if her glasses are forever. Best answer? | Prescriptions change as she grows, so not always the same ones | Yes, forever the same | No, eyes fix by age 10 | Only if she eats carrots
test: In a cartoon the nerdy kid with glasses is always the loser. What's true? | Glasses are common and say nothing about who you are | Glasses make you clever | Glasses make you weak | Only nerds need glasses
exam: Identify the condition wherein light is brought to focus anterior to the retina, rendering distant objects indistinct. | Myopia, or short sight | Amblyopia, or lazy eye | Cataract | Colour blindness
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the habitual wearing of spectacles by children. | It does not cause deterioration of the eyesight | It causes deterioration of the eyesight | It induces a permanent squint | It is prohibited by law before the age of sixteen
exam: Ascertain the purpose for which the occlusion of the stronger eye is prescribed. | To compel the brain to employ the weaker eye | To protect the stronger eye from any infection | To correct colour blindness | To dilate the pupil
exam: Determine the treatment ordinarily employed in cases of cataract. | Surgical replacement of the clouded lens with an artificial lens | The application of an eye patch | Dietary supplementation with carrots | No treatment exists
